&lt;p&gt;Five tiers, one direction of flow: the signals banks already send → read-only ingestion → a deterministic reconciliation engine → a ledger where every real event is exactly one row → analytics on top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backend:&lt;/strong&gt; Python 3.12, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy 2, Alembic, PostgreSQL, pdfplumber&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend:&lt;/strong&gt; React 18, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind, Recharts&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infra:&lt;/strong&gt; Render (API), Neon (Postgres), Vercel (dashboard)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  1. One transaction, many sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The key modelling decision: a financial event and the &lt;em&gt;evidence&lt;/em&gt; for it are different things.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's one &lt;code&gt;transactions&lt;/code&gt; table and a separate &lt;code&gt;transaction_sources&lt;/code&gt; table. When the same payment arrives via email &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; SMS &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; statement, those become three &lt;strong&gt;sources&lt;/strong&gt; attached to &lt;strong&gt;one&lt;/strong&gt; transaction — never three transactions. Each extra source raises the record's confidence and can fill in blank fields.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Deterministic fingerprints
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every parsed event gets a SHA-256 fingerprint built from:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;provider + type + amount + currency + account_last4 + reference + rounded time bucket
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Ingestion then resolves to one of four outcomes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Outcome&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Action&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;unique&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;create a new transaction&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;exact_duplicate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;same fingerprint → attach the source only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;probable_duplicate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;same money, close in time → attach, raise confidence, enrich fields&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;needs_review&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;similar but conflicting reference → &lt;strong&gt;keep both&lt;/strong&gt; and flag&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last rule matters: when in doubt, FinSync never silently deletes money. Re-processing the same email or SMS is a no-op, guarded by unique source IDs and content hashes — so every sync is idempotent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Reconciling alerts with statements
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fingerprints can't catch the alert-vs-statement case, because the statement has a different date and often no reference. A separate reconciliation pass handles it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;match by &lt;strong&gt;reference&lt;/strong&gt; when both sides have one (strong signal),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;otherwise the &lt;strong&gt;nearest time within a few days&lt;/strong&gt;, same direction and amount,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;keep the &lt;strong&gt;statement row&lt;/strong&gt; (it carries the authoritative running balance),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;enrich it with the alert's merchant and exact time, repoint the alert's source onto it, and drop the duplicate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result: balances that reconcile to the rupee, with the rich details from the real-time alerts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1hrsie12vbw6gjexjq2y.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1hrsie12vbw6gjexjq2y.png" alt="Unified ledger" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Analytics and forecasting
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once the ledger is clean, the fun part is easy:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;per-account balances anchored on the newest statement running balance,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;income/expense flows that &lt;strong&gt;exclude internal transfers&lt;/strong&gt; between my own accounts,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;"learn-once" categorization — re-categorize one merchant and every matching row follows,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a Monte-Carlo balance forecast for the next few months.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkjukl5j4wbbhzdgt31kn.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkjukl5j4wbbhzdgt31kn.png" alt="Forecast" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Security: read-only by design
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Handling bank data means being strict about what &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; to do:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gmail access is &lt;strong&gt;read-only OAuth&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;gmail.readonly&lt;/code&gt;), nothing else.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No scraping of banking apps, no private APIs, never stores passwords, OTPs, PINs or card numbers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Raw SMS text isn't persisted — only normalized fields plus a one-way hash for dedup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The SMS webhook is authenticated with HMAC-SHA256 and timestamp anti-replay.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I learned
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model evidence separately from facts.&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;code&gt;transaction_sources&lt;/code&gt; split made every later feature simpler.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Make ingestion idempotent from day one.&lt;/strong&gt; Being able to re-run any sync safely is what makes debugging bearable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Prefer "keep both and flag" over clever merging.&lt;/strong&gt; A false merge is far worse than a visible duplicate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test parsers with real (redacted) samples.&lt;/strong&gt; The test suite (71 tests) is mostly parser, dedup and reconciliation cases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Try the demo at &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://finsync-green.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://finsync-green.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and have a look at the code on &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/danishzulfiqar/FinSync" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. I'd love feedback — especially on the reconciliation strategy. How do &lt;em&gt;you&lt;/em&gt; deal with the same transaction showing up in multiple places?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Context API is a react feature used to share data between multiple components without using props methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By using props method here comes the issue named props drilling ,shows that the same data have pass through each component, if some components doesn't need that data but also have access of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So to avoid this we using context &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How Context API works&lt;br&gt;
Context API mainly involves three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;createContext() – Creates a context.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provider – Makes data available to child components.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;useContext() – Allows components to access that data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've run a test from a visual editor, you've probably seen it: the page loads with the old headline, and a beat later the new one snaps into place. That's flicker. Client-side testing tools all have to deal with it, because their script applies the variant after the browser has started painting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From inside the browser you can either show the original and swap it, so the visitor sees the flash, or hide the page until the script is done. The second option is the "anti-flicker snippet", and most tools turn it on by default.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How long the page stays hidden
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An anti-flicker snippet makes the page invisible, usually with &lt;code&gt;opacity: 0&lt;/code&gt; on the body or a class like Google Optimize's &lt;code&gt;async-hide&lt;/code&gt;. It reveals the page when the testing script finishes or a timeout fires, whichever comes first, so the timeout is your worst case. The defaults are generous:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Google Optimize&lt;/strong&gt; shipped its snippet with a &lt;strong&gt;4,000 ms&lt;/strong&gt; default (&lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/optimize" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google's developer docs&lt;/a&gt;). Optimize shut down on September 30, 2023, but copies of that snippet are still sitting in plenty of &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Webflow Optimize&lt;/strong&gt; also defaults to &lt;strong&gt;4,000 ms&lt;/strong&gt;. Enterprise plans can set anything from 0 to 30,000 ms, and Webflow's help page warns that lower values bring back flicker (&lt;a href="https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/52616936868115-Anti-flicker-prevention-in-Optimize" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Webflow help&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Optimizely recommends loading its snippet &lt;strong&gt;synchronously&lt;/strong&gt; in the head instead (&lt;a href="https://support.optimizely.com/hc/en-us/articles/4410289847053" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Optimizely support&lt;/a&gt;), which blocks rendering until the script has downloaded and run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On fast wifi with a warm cache, the script usually wins and the page appears within a few hundred milliseconds. On a slow phone, your visitor waits for the timeout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it does to Core Web Vitals
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Largest Contentful Paint can't happen while the page is invisible, so LCP absorbs the whole wait. Vercel's guide on A/B testing and Core Web Vitals says the failsafe commonly sits at 4 seconds and LCP takes all of it (&lt;a href="https://vercel.com/kb/guide/ecommerce-ab-testing-core-web-vitals" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Vercel KB&lt;/a&gt;). Google rates LCP as good at 2.5 seconds or less, measured at the 75th percentile (&lt;a href="https://web.dev/articles/vitals" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;web.dev&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Published measurements are rare. DebugBear tested one site and recorded an LCP of about 6.0 seconds with body hiding and about 2.7 seconds with the hiding overridden (&lt;a href="https://www.debugbear.com/blog/ab-testing-anti-flicker-body-hiding" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DebugBear&lt;/a&gt;). That's one site, and your numbers will differ, but they'll move in the same direction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same Vercel guide also covers layout shift. Without anti-flicker, swapping content in can push CLS past 0.1. With it, CLS can look healthy for the wrong reason, since the browser doesn't record shifts that happen while the page is hidden. A clean CLS score on a tested page might only mean nobody could see the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The browser team's own advice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome's guidance on flicker-free client-side testing suggests &lt;code&gt;blocking="render"&lt;/code&gt; with a strict execution budget of under 100 ms, says not to combine it with anti-flicker snippets, and recommends testing on the server wherever you can (&lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/performance/flicker-free-client-side-ab-testing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chrome for Developers&lt;/a&gt;). The people who build the browser think the cleanest fix is to stop deciding in the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Deciding before the first byte
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Next.js server already renders the page, so it can render the right variant from the start. You can build that yourself in about 100 lines; our &lt;a href="https://evolveab.com/guides/nextjs-ab-testing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;guide to A/B testing in the App Router&lt;/a&gt; shows how. evolveAB does the assignment in the Next.js proxy, which reads a signed config bundle from memory (no network call), sets a sticky cookie and rewrites the request to that variant's cached render. The HTML arrives with the variant already in it, so there's nothing to hide or swap and no testing script blocking the head.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That has a cost too. Our code in the proxy takes 0.1 to 0.35 ms, but on Vercel any proxy adds roughly 20 ms to a first page view; one that does nothing measured 18 to 23 ms in our tests. Twenty milliseconds before the first byte is an easy trade against up to four seconds of blank page, as long as nobody pretends it's zero.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We also measure each variant's page speed in visitors' browsers and compare it with control. Once there are enough page views to be sure, auto-rollback pauses a variant that's clearly slower: LCP 20% worse, CLS up by 0.05 or INP 50 ms slower. A variant shouldn't win on sign-ups by quietly making the page slower.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Measuring it on your own site
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need to switch tools to see what anti-flicker costs you. Run a throttled Lighthouse or WebPageTest trace on a tested page and look at the gap between first byte and first paint, then find the timeout in your snippet (it's usually a plain number in the code). If it says 4000 and your mobile LCP hovers around 2.5 seconds, you know where part of it goes.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on the &lt;a href="https://evolveab.com/blog/the-anti-flicker-tax" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Evolve blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Email verification looks like a small feature until a user clicks “send again” while a request is still running, opens an old link, or returns to the tab after the token expired. Then a simple &lt;code&gt;isVerified&lt;/code&gt; boolean starts carrying too much meaning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have found a cleaner approach in React projects: model the verification journey as a small, typed state machine. It gives the UI a clear answer to “what can happen next?” and gives tests a stable contract. The result is less spinner guessing, fewer impossible screens, and a signup flow that feels intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why a boolean is too small for email verification
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is tempting:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isVerified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setIsVerified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;But &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; could mean all of these things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The user has not submitted the form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The verification request is in flight.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The email was sent successfully.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The request failed temporarily.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The link was invalid or already used.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The link expired.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those states need different copy, controls, and recovery actions. A user should not see a generic “not verified” message when the real next step is to check an inbox. The form dont need to know every backend detail, but it does need a trustworthy product state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also where test data gets messy. A fixture containing &lt;code&gt;temp gamil com&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;dummy e mail&lt;/code&gt; should be treated as an input value, not as a special UI mode. Keep data validation separate from the state model so odd addresses do not create hidden branches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Model the flow as a typed state union
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In TypeScript, a discriminated union makes each state explicit:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sentAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the renderer has a finite set of cases. If the backend later adds a “blocked” response, the compiler can help find every UI branch that needs a decision. That is more useful than allowing a new string to flow through and silently render the wrong panel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reducer can stay small:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Action&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"submit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sentAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduceVerification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"submit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sentAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sentAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The reducer does not call the API. It only describes how known events change the screen. That separation keeps the component easier to read, and it makes transition tests quick.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep the React UI honest during async work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A state union is useful only if the UI respects it. Render actions from the current state rather than sprinkling independent loading and error flags through the component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"polite"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;link&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"polite"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Check&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;link&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ResendButton&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;You&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;can&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpiredNotice&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ErrorNotice&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Disable duplicate actions while &lt;code&gt;sending&lt;/code&gt;, but do not disable the entire page. Keyboard focus should land on the next useful control when the state changes. An accessible status message are often more helpful than a full-screen spinner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also decide what happens when an old request finishes after a newer one. Attach a request ID or use an abort controller so a slow response cannot move a newer &lt;code&gt;sent&lt;/code&gt; state back to &lt;code&gt;failed&lt;/code&gt;. Race conditions is easier to prevent at the event boundary than to patch in the JSX.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test transitions with isolated email fixtures
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Test the state transitions first, then add a smaller number of component tests for accessible copy and controls. The important cases are not just “success” and “failure”; they are the edges between states.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;it&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"shows the inbox state after the send event"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduceVerification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dev@example.test"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dev@example.test"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sentAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1700000000000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dev@example.test"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;sentAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1700000000000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Use a fresh disposable email fixture per test run when the flow requires an actual message. Do not share one inbox across parallel tests: one test can consume the link while another is still waiting. The same principle applies to password reset and OAuth scenarios; &lt;a href="https://dev.to/sophiax99/oauth-email-verification-needs-a-proof-carrying-test-oda"&gt;proof-carrying verification tests&lt;/a&gt; make the causal chain visible instead of relying on timing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For API-backed suites, give each test a run identifier and clean up messages after the assertion. &lt;a href="https://dev.to/kevindev27/testing-password-reset-emails-in-postgresql-backed-rest-apis-fb8"&gt;Isolated signup inboxes&lt;/a&gt; help keep a passing test from accidentally reading another test's email. It sounds basic, but shared inboxes create flakes that are very hard to reproduce locally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A quick review checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before shipping an email verification screen, ask:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can every visible state be named in one TypeScript union?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does each state have one clear next action?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can an old request overwrite a newer state?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are resend, expired-link, and invalid-link paths tested?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does the UI announce async status without stealing focus?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does every parallel test own its email fixture?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the answers are clear, the feature usually becomes simpler to change. New backend responses have a place to go, and the UI is less likely to show an impossible combination of loading, success, and error messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Common questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Should I use a state machine library?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not necessarily. A discriminated union and a reducer are enough for a compact flow. Consider a library when the number of states, guarded transitions, or long-running effects makes the rules hard to see in one file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Should verification state live in global state?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Usually no. Keep it local to the signup or account flow until another screen genuinely needs it. Global state makes stale email and token data easier to retain than intended.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is a disposable email fixture safe in production tests?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can be appropriate for controlled test accounts, but keep production customer data out of shared test inboxes. Define retention and cleanup rules, and make the fixture boundary obvious to the team.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Typed states are a small design choice, but they pay off quickly. The UI explains what is happening, tests can target real transitions, and the next React feature does not need to reverse-engineer a pile of booleans. That is a better verification flow for developers and users both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the Events list, the browser discovered &lt;strong&gt;zero&lt;/strong&gt; WebMCP tools. Opening an event draft exposed &lt;strong&gt;four&lt;/strong&gt;. Switching to a trip draft replaced them with &lt;strong&gt;five trip tools&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That route sequence is the behavior I wanted when &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Trip Workbench&lt;/a&gt; gained a second workflow. Registering every operation at startup would have exposed two unrelated domains even when only one editor was visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Page-scoped tools make the current context clearer. They also create a design question: how does an agent enter a workflow whose tools exist only after the right page is open?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep domain identity in the contract
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The event editor has a title, date range, time zone, location mode, location label, and notes. The trip editor has airports, travel dates, cabin, traveler count, and purpose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both workflows reuse authorization, revision, and command-receipt patterns, but they keep separate coordinators, services, tables, validation rules, policy versions, and identifiers. Event tools accept &lt;code&gt;eventId&lt;/code&gt;; trip tools accept &lt;code&gt;draftId&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The event editor exposes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Purpose&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;get_event_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read saved fields, revision, status, and unsaved-change state&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;update_event_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Apply an allowed patch to an expected revision&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;validate_event_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Return field issues and policy findings&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;prepare_event_review&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Show findings while the event remains a draft&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This synthetic example does not send invitations, connect to calendars, or book venues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Register when the editor has context
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The React editor registers tools only after it has a draft identity and status. A condensed lifecycle looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;eventId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;draftStatus&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"draft"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;registration&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AbortController&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;registerEventDraftTools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventCoordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;registration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;onStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setRegistration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;registration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;eventId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;draftStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;eventCoordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/imperative-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chrome’s current imperative API&lt;/a&gt; documents this signal-based lifecycle. As of Chrome 153, aborting the registration removes the tool without cancelling an already-running execution. That is helpful for component cleanup, but it means route cleanup and in-flight operation safety are separate responsibilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The draft identity belongs in the dependency list. React may retain the same component type while moving from one event to another; the old tools must disappear and the new handlers must bind to the new event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test the exact route sequence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The native browser run signed in normally, used visible navigation, created a synthetic event draft, and inspected the browser’s real tool collection. It did not call the coordinator directly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Context&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Discovered interface&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traveler on Trips list&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;none&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traveler on Events list&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;none&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traveler editing an event&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;four event tools&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traveler editing a trip&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;five trip tools&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traveler back on Events list&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;none&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traveler reopening the event&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;four event tools&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Observer editing the event&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;read, validate, prepare&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Observer back on Events list&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;none&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An exact-name assertion catches stale registrations:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expected&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"get_event_draft"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"prepare_event_review"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"update_event_draft"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"validate_event_draft"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;actual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;webmcp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;deepEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;actual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The observer result is useful but limited. Omitting a mutation tool reduces accidental exposure; it does not authorize anything. The server must still reject writes from an actor without permission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Execute a useful operation, not just discovery
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tool names alone do not prove that the page contract works. The run updated a synthetic workshop, validated it, and prepared an inline review.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Customer engineering workshop"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"startDate"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-25"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"endDate"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-26"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"timeZone"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"America/Los_Angeles"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"locationMode"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"manual"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"locationLabel"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Demo workshop room"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"notes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Synthetic example; no invitations"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The accepted update moved the draft from revision 1 to 2. &lt;code&gt;prepare_event_review&lt;/code&gt; displayed a &lt;code&gt;MANUAL_LOCATION&lt;/code&gt; requirement with &lt;code&gt;canSubmit: true&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;submitted: false&lt;/code&gt;. The visible title matched an independent database query, and the event remained a draft with zero submissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The injected test clock was October 14, 2026, so the dates were still in the future. Pinning time matters when validation depends on dates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A narrow interface needs a way in
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Events list intentionally exposes no editor tools, so those tools cannot open their own workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The recorded run used the normal Events link and “New event draft” button. A browser agent with navigation and actuation can take a similar path. A client limited to WebMCP calls cannot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two defensible designs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Treat browser navigation as part of the client contract.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add a bounded list-level tool to open an authorized draft or create a new one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would avoid a generic “call any API” or “run arbitrary JavaScript” escape hatch. It destroys the narrow schemas and server checks that make the editor tools reviewable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Do not turn tool count into a performance claim
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This run proves that the discovered interface changes with route and role. It does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; prove that a model completes tasks faster with four tools than with nine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That requires a separate evaluation: keep the task and executable behavior fixed, vary the visible tool set, and measure wrong-target updates, workflow-entry failures, correct persisted outcomes, recovery steps, and final-answer accuracy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current evidence supports a narrower conclusion: expose editing tools where the relevant object is visible, remove them when the context ends, preserve server authorization, and design an explicit entry path for the clients you actually support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quem já trabalhou em consultoria conhece esta cena.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;O cliente demora semanas para mandar os requisitos: o texto do e-mail, o acesso ao sistema, o layout da landing page. Quando finalmente manda tudo, espera que esteja pronto &lt;strong&gt;no dia seguinte&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A gente chamava isso de &lt;strong&gt;fritar pastel&lt;/strong&gt;. O pedido chega, e o cliente espera que saia quentinho na hora, como na feira.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A regra que ninguém escreve no contrato
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Em TI, entrega sob pressão tem uma regra simples, que todo mundo conhece e quase ninguém fala em voz alta:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Opção&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;O que você ganha&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;O que você paga&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Entregar rápido&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;cumpre o prazo que o cliente espera&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;abre mão de testes, de acabamento, de validar o que não deu tempo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Entregar completo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;entrega o que foi combinado, do jeito certo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;estoura o prazo (o SLA) que o cliente tinha na cabeça&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Não existe a terceira opção, "rápido e completo". Quando parece que existe, é porque alguma coisa ficou escondida. E foi exatamente isso que aconteceu nesta história.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  O atalho: mockar a API
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Numa consultoria em que trabalhei, pegamos um desses pastéis. Era um front em &lt;strong&gt;Next.js&lt;/strong&gt; que consumia as APIs do cliente para montar as telas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;O problema: o cliente demorou para nos passar as APIs que ele usava para consultar o serviço. E o prazo de entrega não mudou.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A decisão foi a que quase todo time toma nessa situação: &lt;strong&gt;mockar as APIs&lt;/strong&gt;. Criamos respostas falsas com o formato que esperávamos, construímos as telas em cima delas e combinamos o seguinte: quando as APIs reais chegassem, era só apontar as requisições para os endereços certos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Na teoria, trocar o mock pela API real era mudar uma URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Na prática, o mock tinha um defeito que só aparece tarde demais: &lt;strong&gt;ele responde o que você imagina, não o que a API real devolve.&lt;/strong&gt; O formato até pode bater. O volume, quase nunca.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A virada: a API real chegou um dia antes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Como era de se esperar, o cliente entregou os dados das APIs &lt;strong&gt;faltando um dia para a entrega final&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quando finalmente apontamos o front para a API real, veio a surpresa:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Eram muito mais dados do que o sistema aguentava.&lt;/strong&gt; O mock devolvia uma lista pequena e bem comportada. A API real devolvia tudo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A API não tinha paginação.&lt;/strong&gt; Não havia como pedir "os primeiros 20". Cada chamada trazia a lista inteira.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;O front ficou absurdamente lento.&lt;/strong&gt; O React recebia muito mais dados do que a tela mostrava e tentava renderizar tudo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Começaram os erros de hidratação.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Esse último merece uma explicação rápida.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  O que é erro de hidratação
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No Next.js, a página é renderizada primeiro no servidor, que manda o HTML pronto para o navegador. Depois, o React "acorda" esse HTML no navegador e passa a controlar a página. Esse processo se chama &lt;strong&gt;hidratação&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para dar certo, o React precisa gerar no navegador &lt;strong&gt;exatamente o mesmo HTML&lt;/strong&gt; que o servidor gerou. Se alguma coisa diferir, ele reclama do erro de hidratação, e a página pode piscar, perder interatividade ou ser refeita do zero no navegador.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Com uma lista enorme, componentes pesados e dados que podiam mudar entre a renderização no servidor e a no navegador, as chances de diferença se multiplicam. Junte isso com a lentidão, e a tela que funcionava lindamente com o mock virou outra coisa.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;E faltavam &lt;strong&gt;3 horas&lt;/strong&gt; para o deploy e a apresentação para o cliente.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  O que fizemos em 3 horas
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Com esse prazo, não dava para consertar a causa: a API era do cliente, e não ia ganhar paginação até o fim da tarde. O objetivo passou a ser outro: &lt;strong&gt;entregar algo que funcionasse na apresentação, sabendo que seria provisório.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Paginação no front
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Se a API não pagina, o front pagina. A API continuava devolvendo tudo, mas a tela passou a renderizar só um pedaço por vez:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;POR_PAGINA&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ListaPaginada&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;itens&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;itens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pagina&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setPagina&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;visiveis&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;itens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pagina&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;POR_PAGINA&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pagina&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;POR_PAGINA&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;totalPaginas&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ceil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;itens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;POR_PAGINA&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visiveis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Linha&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Paginacao&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;atual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pagina&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;totalPaginas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setPagina&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Isso não resolve o tamanho da resposta: os dados ainda trafegam inteiros pela rede. Mas resolve o que estava travando a tela, que era &lt;strong&gt;renderizar&lt;/strong&gt; tudo de uma vez.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Simplificar componentes e renderizações
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A segunda medida foi cortar o que deixava cada item pesado e o que podia gerar diferença entre servidor e navegador:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;componentes visuais mais simples, com menos camadas e menos efeitos;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;menos lógica dentro da renderização de cada item da lista;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;nada que dependesse do navegador sendo calculado durante a primeira renderização.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;O custo apareceu na hora: &lt;strong&gt;a tela ficou visualmente mais simples do que o layout que tínhamos apresentado.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A apresentação: saiu a tempo, mas o cliente percebeu
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;O deploy saiu e a apresentação aconteceu no horário. Funcionou.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mas o cliente notou que a tela tinha elementos visuais mais simples do que o combinado. Explicamos o que tinha acontecido: foi um problema técnico, causado pelo volume de dados da API, e na semana seguinte teríamos a tela &lt;strong&gt;pixel perfect&lt;/strong&gt;, do jeito que estava no Figma, que era o que tínhamos combinado.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Repare no que aconteceu aqui. O pastel foi frito no prazo, mas parte da conta ficou para a semana seguinte. Isso é &lt;strong&gt;dívida técnica&lt;/strong&gt; na forma mais pura: uma decisão consciente de entregar menos agora para cumprir o prazo, com o compromisso de pagar depois.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;O problema não é ter feito dívida. Às vezes, é a decisão certa. O problema é quando ela é feita &lt;strong&gt;sem ninguém saber&lt;/strong&gt;, e o mock tinha escondido a dívida de nós mesmos até o último dia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  O que mudamos depois
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lição que ficou não foi técnica. Foi de processo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A partir desse projeto, começamos a pedir a &lt;strong&gt;todos os nossos clientes&lt;/strong&gt; que atendessem às nossas &lt;strong&gt;especificações técnicas&lt;/strong&gt; antes de começarmos. Ou seja: o que a gente precisa receber, e em que condições, para conseguir entregar no prazo que eles querem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Se eu fosse montar essa lista hoje, ela teria pelo menos isto:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Prazo para entregar as APIs&lt;/strong&gt;, com data, e não "assim que der". Se a API atrasar, a entrega atrasa junto, e isso fica escrito desde o começo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Uma amostra real da resposta&lt;/strong&gt;, com o volume de produção ou pelo menos o tamanho esperado. Não o formato: o tamanho.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Paginação&lt;/strong&gt; (ou filtros) em qualquer endpoint que devolva listas.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ambiente de homologação&lt;/strong&gt; com dados parecidos com os de produção.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Como usar mock sem cair nessa armadilha
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mockar não foi o erro. Mock é uma ferramenta ótima para não ficar parado esperando o outro lado. O erro foi confiar que o mock representava a realidade. Algumas formas de evitar isso:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mock com volume, não só com formato.&lt;/strong&gt; Se o mock devolve 5 itens, você está testando 5 itens. Gere milhares:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;faker&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@faker-js/faker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;itensMock&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;faker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;nome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;faker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;commerce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;productName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;descricao&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;faker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lorem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;paragraph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Se a tela travar com 10 mil itens no mock, ela vai travar em produção. A diferença é que você descobre na primeira semana, não na última tarde.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pergunte o tamanho antes de perguntar o formato.&lt;/strong&gt; "Quantos registros essa chamada devolve hoje? E daqui a um ano?" é uma pergunta de 30 segundos que teria mudado essa história inteira.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Trate a troca do mock pela API real como uma tarefa, não como um detalhe.&lt;/strong&gt; "É só trocar a URL" é a frase mais perigosa do projeto. Reserve tempo para ela no cronograma, de preferência longe da véspera da entrega.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Deixe a dívida visível.&lt;/strong&gt; Quando o atalho for necessário, escreva o que ficou para trás e combine com o cliente quando será pago. Uma dívida que o cliente conhece é um combinado. Uma dívida que ele descobre na apresentação vira desconfiança.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist para o próximo pastel
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Peça as APIs com data de entrega, e amarre o prazo do projeto a essa data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Peça uma amostra real da resposta, com o volume de produção.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Confirme se os endpoints de lista têm paginação ou filtros.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Monte o mock com volume realista, não só com o formato certo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Reserve tempo no cronograma para trocar o mock pela API real.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Se precisar de um atalho, escreva a dívida e combine com o cliente quando ela será paga.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusão
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fritar pastel faz parte da vida em consultoria, e nem sempre dá para fugir. O que dá para escolher é &lt;strong&gt;onde fica o risco&lt;/strong&gt;: escondido num mock que só responde o que você imagina, ou na mesa, combinado com o cliente desde o começo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Naquela tarde, a gente entregou no prazo e pagou a conta depois. Hoje, a primeira pergunta que eu faço quando alguém me passa uma API é: &lt;strong&gt;quantos dados ela devolve?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;E você? Já teve um pastel que queimou na véspera da entrega? Conta nos comentários! 🚀&lt;/p&gt;




